## Part 2: A bootstrap interval

::: exercise
Build a bootstrap distribution of your primary coefficient in `add_fit`. Fill in the model formula, delete `#| eval: false`, and run it. If your primary variable is categorical, the coefficient you want may be the second one, `coef(fit)[2]`, or whichever row matches your comparison.
:::

::: answer

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
set.seed(2026)
boot <- do(1000) * {
  fit <- lm(y ~ x1 + x2, data = resample(pilot))
  tibble(estimate = coef(fit)[2])
}
```
:::

:::

::: exercise
Make a histogram of your bootstrapped estimates and mark your original estimate on it. Then use `quantile(boot$estimate, c(0.025, 0.975))` to get the 95% **bootstrap interval**. How does it compare with the formula interval from Part 1? Why might they differ when you only have about 20 islanders?
:::

::: answer

